WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.959
Speaker 1: It's great to have back on the
program today Rick Doblin, who's the founder

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:10.250
and executive director of MAPS, the Multidisciplinary
Association for Psychedelic Studies, which

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:17.430
researches legal developing legal contexts
for the careful and beneficial uses of psychedelics

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.430
and marijuana.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.240
Rick, it's great to have you back on today.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.220
Speaker 4: Yes, it's my pleasure.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.800
I look forward to our discussion, so
Speaker 1: I want to eventually get to what

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.380
some of the advancements and developments
have been since you and I last spoke a couple

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.119
of years ago.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:38.140
But first, you mentioned that you were recently
in Texas with a bunch of Republicans, including

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:45.260
Dan Crenshaw, who is not known typically as
the progressive beacon of hope to the left.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.699
But there seem to be some sort of camaraderie
on the issue of using psychedelics for for

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:52.539
veterans and in other contexts.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:59.170
Speaker 4: Yeah, well, Representative Crenshaw
is a former Navy SEAL, right, and he's heard

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:05.080
now from quite a number of Navy SEALs who
have PTSD, traumatic brain injury, and the

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.030
treatments that they've been getting from
the VA haven't been helping them.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:14.640
And so there's a group called Vets by Amber
and Marcus Kapone that brings particularly

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.390
Navy SEALs down to Mexico for ibogaine, which
is a psychedelic drug from Western Africa,

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.720
and also five MDMA, which is a drug from the
Sonoran toad.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:29.000
It's the most powerful of all the psychedelics.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.080
And these Navy SEALs have been finding a lot
of relief and other veterans as well.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.250
And so they've been sharing the news with
Dan Crenshaw.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.540
And also he's been hearing with some of the
veterans and others in our studies with MDMA

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.420
for PTSD.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:50.110
And so now he has become a major advocate
and he's working of all bipartisan way with.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:55.920
Tim Ryan, who is the congressman from Ohio,
friend of the show.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.080
Speaker 1: Very good guy.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.030
Speaker 4: Yeah, he's fantastic.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.920
So that the two of them, though, are working
together.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.760
We're getting ready to launch a bill to give
twenty five million to the Department of Defense

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.690
for psychedelic research for PTSD.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.080
And then another bill to give twenty five
million to the VA.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.430
And so what we're finding is that the healing
of veterans and others with trauma from trauma

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.239
is bipartisan.

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:27.969
And one of the things that was so interesting
at the discussion with Dan Crenshaw is here.

00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:34.099
I am surrounded by Texas Republicans and I
talked about how maps as a nonprofit, we've

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:39.769
raised over one hundred and fifteen million
dollars in donations to do this research.

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:45.319
Over our thirty five years and I started out
by saying that we've got this bipartisan support

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:50.870
and I talked about Rebecca Mercer, who was
the family big supporters of Trump and Bannon

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.560
owned Cambridge Analytica.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.010
We know about scraping all the data from Facebook.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:03:00.299
I said we have support from Rebecca Mercer,
from Elizabeth Koch, whose father is Charles

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:07.230
Koch, and we have support from the Rockefeller
family, the Buffett family, the Pritzker was.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:11.019
And I said and goes all the way to George
Soros, who also supports us.

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:15.810
And there was kind of this sort of initial
kind of joke or groan that Dan Cranshaw made

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.400
about George Soros.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.579
But in the end, it was like it was like we're
all in it together.

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:24.069
That was the dominant message.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.900
There was one thing that was really surprising
to me, too, which was afterwards there was

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:35.499
a woman who's a therapist, used to be a therapist
at the Houston VA, and she does what's called

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:40.140
MDR, which is an approach towards helping
people with PTSD.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.750
And we talked about her husband was a veteran
and she's helped him with PTSD.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.819
She's very interested in our research.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:49.400
And then she said she's actually running for
Congress.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.690
I said, that's really good.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.499
And then she said, yeah, I'm running against
a Republican.

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:54.129
I said, all right.

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:55.279
So we talked.

00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:57.930
It was like we exchanged cards.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.139
It was very interesting.

00:03:59.139 --> 00:04:03.709
And then I get home to write her a little
email and I discover that she's running as

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:08.059
a Republican against a Republican who's not
sufficiently right wing.

00:04:08.059 --> 00:04:09.400
She's right.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.450
Currently existing
Speaker 1: Republican.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:12.819
But she likes what you're doing.

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:16.139
Speaker 4: She likes what we're doing because
she likes healing veterans.

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:23.960
So somehow or other able to take psychedelics
and healing of trauma and move it out of this

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.150
partizan struggle.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.740
And it seems like it's going pretty well in
that way.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.180
Speaker 1: Yeah, it's it's an incredible thing.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:36.470
And I mean, I would be curious to hear, since
we last talked, there were so many things

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.470
you were researching.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.919
There were so many things that you were studying.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:45.410
What are the sort of tangible advancements
that have been made in this field of using

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:51.419
psychedelics and as well as sometimes cannabis
for for a lot of different situations, wellbeing,

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:52.419
et cetera?

00:04:52.419 --> 00:04:54.820
What advancements have we made in the last
couple of years?

00:04:54.820 --> 00:05:01.479
Speaker 4: Well, in May, we published We Meaning
Maps that we published the results from our

00:05:01.479 --> 00:05:05.860
first Phase three study of MDMA assisted psychotherapy
for PTSD.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.229
You have to do two phase three studies to
get a drug approved by FDA and they have to

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:12.270
prove safety and efficacy.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:16.860
And the first study was published in Nature
Medicine, which is one of the most highest

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.960
impact scientific journals in the world.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.419
And we had outstanding results better than
we even had hoped.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:29.300
And so what we showed it was highly significant
results, statistically significant, large

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.229
effect size, excellent safety record.

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:38.759
The most amazing part is we work with the
hardest cases, meaning severe PTSD, chronic

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:40.759
PTSD.

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:45.800
Unlike many PTSD studies, we enroll people
who have previously attempted suicide.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.580
And what we showed is that two months after
the last experimental session, two thirds

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.500
of these people no longer had PTSD.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:54.919
It's just astounding.

00:05:54.919 --> 00:05:57.550
The control group was thirty two percent.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.120
That's the group that got therapy without
MDMA.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.690
So 30 to almost one third no longer had PTSD
from our therapy.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:09.580
But when you add MDMA to the therapy, it's
sixty seven percent no longer have PTSD.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:14.590
And of the one third that still has PTSD,
most of them had what's called clinically

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.689
significant reductions of PTSD symptoms.

00:06:17.689 --> 00:06:22.330
So we are the only group that's in phase three
with psychedelics.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:28.129
There's now about one hundred plus psychedelic
companies, for profit companies, a few nonprofits,

00:06:28.129 --> 00:06:31.520
and they're either in earlier stages of the
research or they're trying to develop new

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.630
molecules that they can patent new psychedelics.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.449
But we're the only ones in phase three.

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:38.110
So that's the main development.

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:42.150
There's also groups that have been working
with psilocybin and they're doing well in

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.250
phase two.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:50.639
And it's psilocybin for depression, psilocybin
for nicotine addiction, psilocybin for alcoholism,

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:55.580
psilocybin for obsessive compulsive disorder.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:07:02.900
We've also completed studies with psilocybin
excuse me, with MDMA for social anxiety, for

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.380
people anxious about life threatening illnesses.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:10.389
There was an incredible study done by others
that we helped with David not and then says

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:13.570
in England, MDMA for alcohol use disorder.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.689
What it turns out is that trauma is what drives
a lot of drug addiction.

00:07:16.689 --> 00:07:21.669
If you help people deal with the traumas of
life, then they don't need to run away and

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:22.669
drugs.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:24.639
And so we were able to show good results in
that.

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:31.050
There's also starting to be interesting, promising
findings with traumatic brain injury that

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.840
certain psychedelics can be helpful.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:40.319
With that, there's just a whole range of studies
that are now being started with psychedelics

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:43.889
that are really showing enormous progress.

00:07:43.889 --> 00:07:49.759
And one of the main things that we know is
that psychedelics promote what's called neuroplasticity.

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:52.780
And what that means is that our brains are
not fixed.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.229
They evolve as we grow.

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:58.530
We know that when we remember things, there's
something changed in our brain.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:05.280
But more importantly, psychedelics can promote
new neural connections in different kind of

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.280
pathways.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:11.259
So for PTSD, there's been neuroscientists
at Johns Hopkins School Dublin who did work

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:18.159
with MDMA, giving them to octopuses and giving
MDMA to mice for the octopuses.

00:08:18.159 --> 00:08:24.560
They're very solitary creatures and they stay
away from other octopuses unless it's mating

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.849
season, which is the end of their life.

00:08:26.849 --> 00:08:30.940
But you give them MDMA and now they want to
hang out with other octopuses.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:38.039
And so these kind of preverbal deep in our
evolutionary history, MDMA has this profound

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:39.039
impact.

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:43.680
But in mice, when you give MDMA to mice, it
promotes oxytocin release, which is the hormone

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:44.680
of love.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:49.040
They call it the cuddle hormone, the love
nursing mothers.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.640
And it promotes new neural connections, new
growth of of neurons in new pathways.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.029
Do you agree in what happens
Speaker 1: in these studies when the studies

00:09:00.029 --> 00:09:05.160
with people is there like a placebo group,
where do people know whether they're getting

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.360
the MDMA or not?

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.750
Because that seems like it would be a relevant
question in terms of measuring the impact.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:10.750
Right.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:15.910
Speaker 4: Yeah, that's a great question,
my dissertation at the Kennedy School of Government,

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:20.620
where I got my master's and PhD, was on the
regulation of the medical use of psychedelics.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:25.910
And one of the main things I looked at was
how do you do a double blind study with a

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:27.510
drug like a psychedelic?

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:28.510
That's pretty obvious.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:32.589
If you've taken it like you're taking different
pills and you don't notice automatically what

00:09:32.589 --> 00:09:33.589
they do.

00:09:33.589 --> 00:09:37.680
These are very powerful drugs that change
your consciousness and you usually can tell

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:38.680
if that's happened.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:39.680
Sure.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.470
So a lot of my dissertation was about how
to solve this problem.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.079
And I thought I had the solution.

00:09:46.079 --> 00:09:51.630
The solution, I thought was going to be therapy
with low dose MDMA versus therapy with full

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.160
dose MDMA.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:59.459
And the challenge was to find the dose of
MDMA that was high enough that enough people

00:09:59.459 --> 00:10:02.170
would notice it and would not be certain.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:06.500
Was it the low dose or the high dose, but
that it wasn't so high that you started getting

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.560
all the therapeutic potentials of MDMA?

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:12.740
And this is what we explored in phase two.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.600
So maps I started in nineteen eighty six.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.240
We started our first phase one safety study,
nineteen ninety two and in nineteen ninety

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.360
nine in 2000 we started the phase two studies
and that took us till twenty sixteen.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:30.120
But what we discovered and this is I guess
you could say, the beauty and humility of

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:35.870
scientific research is that you discover stuff
that you didn't realize was actually going

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.980
on or you discover that you were wrong.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:44.910
So what I discovered through these studies
and we tested inactive placebo with therapy.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.380
Twenty five milligrams, 30 milligrams, 40
milligrams, 50 milligrams.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:52.250
Seventy five milligrams, one hundred one twenty
five and one fifty.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:57.380
And what we showed is that the therapeutic
dose was lower than we thought.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:03.709
Seventy five milligrams was very effective,
but that the lower doses.

00:11:03.709 --> 00:11:09.120
They did cause a certain amount of inaccurate
guesses, but what they did is they had an

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:15.190
anti therapeutic effect, meaning that everybody
still got better, who got these lower therapy

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:16.560
plus lower doses.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.540
But when we looked at the data, the people
that had therapy without MDMA did better than

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.760
the people that had therapy with low dose
MDMA.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:28.730
And the best way to describe it, it's like
if you're in an airplane and you're about

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:32.620
to take off, sometimes there's turbulence
at the beginning and then you get above the

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.070
clouds and then it's smooth sailing.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.550
So the low doses of MDMA were like this turbulence.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.529
They people were focused on their trauma.

00:11:41.529 --> 00:11:43.200
They were activated a little bit.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:49.910
But the fear reduction properties of MDMA
weren't fully activated, made people uncomfortable.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:56.110
So when we went to the FDA to design phase
three, I open it up by saying that there's

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:59.199
a funny quote by a former president of Harvard.

00:11:59.199 --> 00:12:01.329
And what he said was, never forget.

00:12:01.329 --> 00:12:06.930
There's always a Harvard man on the wrong
side of every issue.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:13.040
And so I said, in this case, it's me, my dissertation,
which fortunately I managed to get my theory

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.329
was wrong and that there is no solution to
the double-blind problem.

00:12:17.329 --> 00:12:21.529
And so he said to the FDA, we can give you
the blinding, but you're going to make it

00:12:21.529 --> 00:12:24.620
easier for us to find a difference between
the two groups.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:29.721
And the real question is, if you can do this
work with therapy without adding a drug, why

00:12:29.721 --> 00:12:31.110
add the drug?

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:36.089
So rather than adding a drug that gives a
little bit of blinding, it's more important

00:12:36.089 --> 00:12:39.790
that we test the therapy by itself where it's
the most effective.

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:43.990
And so the FDA brought in what we like to
joke as the old wise man of the FDA.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:48.630
His name is Bob Temple and he's been there
since nineteen seventy two.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:53.480
And he was the Office of Science Policy and
he came and he said there is he agreed there

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:56.200
is no solution to the double-blind problem.

00:12:56.200 --> 00:13:01.320
And he said that there are other ways to try
to reduce experimenter bias.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:05.161
The double-blind is really to make it so that,
you know, you don't get biased results, that

00:13:05.161 --> 00:13:06.649
you don't find what you want to find.

00:13:06.649 --> 00:13:07.649
Right.

00:13:07.649 --> 00:13:10.690
And so what they first have said is what's
called random assignment.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:15.339
That means everybody is similarly motivated
and then they get randomized either to the

00:13:15.339 --> 00:13:18.519
control group or the experimental group.

00:13:18.519 --> 00:13:25.010
The other thing they said is that the independent
raters who evaluate the outcomes that you

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:28.700
can't have, the therapists say, oh, did this
person get better or not?

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:33.790
Or even the person themselves to to do that,
you need independent raters.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:38.750
And this is done through an hour long interview
with the patient about all of their symptoms.

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.200
And so we have these independent raters and
a very robust system.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:46.519
We have over 20 of them trained by the Boston
Veteran's Administration where they developed

00:13:46.519 --> 00:13:47.569
the measure.

00:13:47.569 --> 00:13:51.019
They do it by random assignment, meaning it's
all telemedicine.

00:13:51.019 --> 00:13:55.820
So they don't know the patient where they
are in the treatment process.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:58.589
They don't know if they've got MDMA or the
placebo.

00:13:58.589 --> 00:14:03.970
So based on that, the FDA said that they would
agree with us that we would do therapy without

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.300
MDMA versus therapy with him.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:11.690
Do you may even still, there's about 10 percent
of the people that are confused about what

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:14.750
they got, which is pretty astonishing.

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:20.240
But sometimes people that resist the emotional
effects of MDMA because they're so scared

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:22.690
of what's going on, they have kind of a muted
effect.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:25.360
So there is a little bit of confusion.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:28.520
But we also negotiated with the European Medicines
Agency.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:33.029
We're trying to negotiate all over the world
to globalize MDMA therapy.

00:14:33.029 --> 00:14:37.520
And so the European Medicines Agency went
through the same analysis and they also agreed

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:44.329
with the FDA that it's therapy without MDMA
versus therapy with MDMA and therapy with

00:14:44.329 --> 00:14:46.069
inactive placebo.

00:14:46.069 --> 00:14:49.250
And we're also doing research in Israel and
Canada.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:50.630
We've done some in Brazil.

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:53.009
We're just moving forward into Australia.

00:14:53.009 --> 00:14:58.810
So I think the frustrating part in a way,
is that there is no good solution to the double-blind

00:14:58.810 --> 00:14:59.810
problem.

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:04.630
But the FDA realizes that that even though
the methodology doesn't work, people are still

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:05.630
suffering.

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:09.190
You still have to do the research and you
still have to design the studies as best you

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:10.190
can.

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:11.190
Right.

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:15.130
So the breakthrough that we had was our first
phase three study being tremendously successful.

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:18.290
